y_paths": {"auto": "/tmp/mte-ng-bankers-fcievqkm/claude-config/projects/-tmp-mte-ng-bankers-fcievqkm-ledgerline/memory/"}, "messaging_socket_path": "/tmp/cc-socks/24738.sock", "fast_mode_state": "off", "fast_mode_disabled_reason": "sdk_opt_in_required", "per_turn_effort_active": true, "view_mode": "default"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbwNTMeN932m1vScFkZT", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (round-half-to-even) is a tie-breaking rule. When a value falls exactly halfway between two candidates, you round to the nearest **even** digit. Anything not exactly halfway rounds normally.\n\n- 2.5 \u2192 2\n- 3.5 \u2192 4\n- 0.125 \u2192 0.12 (to 2 places)\n- 0.135 \u2192 0.14\n- 2.51 \u2192 3 (not a tie, so it rounds normally)\n\n**Why accountants like it:**\n\n1. **It removes upward bias.** Round-half-up always pushes ties up. Over thousands of transactions, that adds a small but systematic overstatement. Round-half-even sends about half the ties up and half down, so the errors cancel out.\n2. **Totals stay closer to the truth.** When you round many line items and sum them, the total drifts less from the sum of the unrounded values.\n3. **It's fair to both parties.** Neither the payer nor the payee is consistently favored by the tie-breaking.\n4. **It's a recognized standard.** IEEE 754 uses it as the default floating-point rounding mode, and many financial and tax regulations specify it. Python's `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN` and .NET's `MidpointRounding.ToEven` implement it.\n\n**Caveats:**\n\n- It only matters for exact ties. With currency stored as `Decimal` or integer cents, ties happen often enough to matter. With binary floats, \"exact\" halves like 0.135 usually aren't representable, so the rule rarely triggers the way you expect.\n- Python's built-in `round()` uses half-even, but on floats. Use `Decimal.quantize(..., rounding=ROUND_HALF_EVEN)` for money.\n- Some jurisdictions or contracts require round-half-up, so check the applicable rules.\n\nThis matches what your project already does: the stored decision says tax amounts here use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, as agreed with finance."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7752, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7752}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7ddffe68-79ec-4ed3-8cd3-1952bb51c3d2", "uuid": "fac7264f-d5c1-41e0-a0e8-0bb8072c33c7", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T17:48:44.080Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbwNT2JmyjLxmVp3u985"}
